<article class="env-lemma" id="07CG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07CG">Lemma <span data-tag="07CG">16.3.3</span>.</a> Let $R \to A$ be a syntomic ring map. Then there exists a smooth $R$-algebra map $A \to C$ with a retraction such that $C$ is a global relative complete intersection over $R$, i.e., </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  C \cong R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ c)  \]
</div>
  <p> flat over $R$ and all fibres of dimension $n - c$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/07CE" data-tag="07CE">16.3.1</a> to get $A \to C$. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07CF" data-tag="07CF">10.136.6</a> we can write $C = R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ c)$ with $f_ i$ mapping to a basis of $J/J^2$. The ring map $R \to C$ is syntomic (hence flat) as it is a composition of a syntomic and a smooth ring map. The dimension of the fibres is $n - c$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00SC" data-tag="00SC">10.135.4</a> (the fibres are local complete intersections, so the lemma applies). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>